{"skill_id":"limit-cycles","version":1,"questions":[{"id":"limit-cycles-r1-q1","version":1,"round":1,"type":"mc","prompt":"A limit cycle is","options":["a closed orbit that nearby trajectories approach or leave","an equilibrium point","any oscillation in a linear system","the same as a center"],"correct_index":0,"explanation":"A limit cycle is an isolated periodic orbit. Nearby trajectories spiral toward (stable) or away from (unstable) it. Equilibrium points are single points, not orbits. Linear systems cannot have isolated limit cycles.","figure":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 280 240\" role=\"img\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\"><title>limit cycle</title><line x1=\"30\" y1=\"120\" x2=\"250\" y2=\"120\"/><polygon points=\"250,120 243.6,122.7 243.6,117.3\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\"/><line x1=\"140\" y1=\"210\" x2=\"140\" y2=\"30\"/><polygon points=\"140,30 142.7,36.4 137.3,36.4\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\"/><text x=\"245\" y=\"136\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">x₁</text><text x=\"148\" y=\"28\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">x₂</text><circle cx=\"140\" cy=\"120\" r=\"60\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 3\"/><polyline points=\"170,120 173.5,114.1 175.1,107.2 174.8,99.9 172.6,92.6 168.6,85.9 163.1,80 156.3,75.2 148.5,71.9 140,70.2 131.2,70.1 122.4,71.8 114,75 106.3,79.8 99.5,86 93.9,93.4 89.8,101.7 87.1,110.7 86.1,120 86.7,129.4 89,138.6 92.9,147.2 98.3,155 104.9,161.8 112.7,167.3 121.3,171.4 130.5,173.9 140,174.8 149.5,174 158.8,171.5 167.4,167.5 175.3,162 182.1,155.3 187.6,147.5 191.6,138.8 194.1,129.5 195,120\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/></svg>","figure_alt":"A closed orbit in the phase plane."},{"id":"limit-cycles-r1-q2","version":1,"round":1,"type":"mc","prompt":"Limit cycles can exist in","options":["only linear systems","only nonlinear systems","any system with feedback","only systems without damping"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Limit cycles require nonlinearity to sustain oscillation. Linear systems have either stable nodes/spirals, centers (non-isolated orbits), or unstable trajectories. The nonlinearity provides energy to maintain the cycle.","figure":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 280 240\" role=\"img\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\"><title>nonlinear cycle</title><line x1=\"30\" y1=\"120\" x2=\"250\" y2=\"120\"/><polygon points=\"250,120 243.6,122.7 243.6,117.3\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\"/><line x1=\"140\" y1=\"210\" x2=\"140\" y2=\"30\"/><polygon points=\"140,30 142.7,36.4 137.3,36.4\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\"/><text x=\"245\" y=\"136\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">x₁</text><text x=\"148\" y=\"28\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">x₂</text><circle cx=\"140\" cy=\"120\" r=\"60\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 3\"/><polyline points=\"170,120 173.5,114.1 175.1,107.2 174.8,99.9 172.6,92.6 168.6,85.9 163.1,80 156.3,75.2 148.5,71.9 140,70.2 131.2,70.1 122.4,71.8 114,75 106.3,79.8 99.5,86 93.9,93.4 89.8,101.7 87.1,110.7 86.1,120 86.7,129.4 89,138.6 92.9,147.2 98.3,155 104.9,161.8 112.7,167.3 121.3,171.4 130.5,173.9 140,174.8 149.5,174 158.8,171.5 167.4,167.5 175.3,162 182.1,155.3 187.6,147.5 191.6,138.8 194.1,129.5 195,120\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/></svg>","figure_alt":"A closed orbit in the phase plane."},{"id":"limit-cycles-r1-q3","version":1,"round":1,"type":"mc","prompt":"A stable limit cycle is approached by","options":["trajectories starting from any initial condition","trajectories from nearby initial conditions","only trajectories exactly on the cycle","no trajectories"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A stable limit cycle is an attractor for nearby trajectories, which spiral in from outside or out from inside. Global stability (from any IC) is not guaranteed. Unstable limit cycles repel nearby trajectories.","figure":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 280 240\" role=\"img\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\"><title>stable cycle</title><line x1=\"30\" y1=\"120\" x2=\"250\" y2=\"120\"/><polygon points=\"250,120 243.6,122.7 243.6,117.3\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\"/><line x1=\"140\" y1=\"210\" x2=\"140\" y2=\"30\"/><polygon points=\"140,30 142.7,36.4 137.3,36.4\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\"/><text x=\"245\" y=\"136\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">x₁</text><text x=\"148\" y=\"28\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">x₂</text><circle cx=\"140\" cy=\"120\" r=\"60\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 3\"/><polyline points=\"170,120 173.5,114.1 175.1,107.2 174.8,99.9 172.6,92.6 168.6,85.9 163.1,80 156.3,75.2 148.5,71.9 140,70.2 131.2,70.1 122.4,71.8 114,75 106.3,79.8 99.5,86 93.9,93.4 89.8,101.7 87.1,110.7 86.1,120 86.7,129.4 89,138.6 92.9,147.2 98.3,155 104.9,161.8 112.7,167.3 121.3,171.4 130.5,173.9 140,174.8 149.5,174 158.8,171.5 167.4,167.5 175.3,162 182.1,155.3 187.6,147.5 191.6,138.8 194.1,129.5 195,120\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/></svg>","figure_alt":"A closed orbit in the phase plane."},{"id":"limit-cycles-r2-q1","version":1,"round":2,"type":"mc","prompt":"The Van der Pol oscillator has","options":["a single stable limit cycle for positive \\(\\mu\\)","no limit cycles","infinitely many limit cycles","only unstable limit cycles"],"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The Van der Pol equation \\(\\ddot{x} - \\mu(1-x^2)\\dot{x} + x = 0\\) has one stable limit cycle for \\(\\mu > 0\\). The nonlinear damping term sustains oscillation by providing negative damping for small amplitude and positive damping for large amplitude.","figure":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 280 240\" role=\"img\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\"><title>Van der Pol</title><line x1=\"30\" y1=\"120\" x2=\"250\" y2=\"120\"/><polygon points=\"250,120 243.6,122.7 243.6,117.3\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\"/><line x1=\"140\" y1=\"210\" x2=\"140\" y2=\"30\"/><polygon points=\"140,30 142.7,36.4 137.3,36.4\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\"/><text x=\"245\" y=\"136\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">x₁</text><text x=\"148\" y=\"28\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">x₂</text><circle cx=\"140\" cy=\"120\" r=\"60\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 3\"/><polyline points=\"170,120 173.5,114.1 175.1,107.2 174.8,99.9 172.6,92.6 168.6,85.9 163.1,80 156.3,75.2 148.5,71.9 140,70.2 131.2,70.1 122.4,71.8 114,75 106.3,79.8 99.5,86 93.9,93.4 89.8,101.7 87.1,110.7 86.1,120 86.7,129.4 89,138.6 92.9,147.2 98.3,155 104.9,161.8 112.7,167.3 121.3,171.4 130.5,173.9 140,174.8 149.5,174 158.8,171.5 167.4,167.5 175.3,162 182.1,155.3 187.6,147.5 191.6,138.8 194.1,129.5 195,120\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/></svg>","figure_alt":"A closed orbit in the phase plane."},{"id":"limit-cycles-r2-q2","version":1,"round":2,"type":"mc","prompt":"A bifurcation is a qualitative change in system behavior as","options":["a parameter varies","time increases","the initial condition changes","the input oscillates"],"correct_index":0,"explanation":"A bifurcation occurs when varying a parameter crosses a threshold that changes the number or stability of equilibria or limit cycles. Time evolution does not cause bifurcation; parameter changes do.","figure":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 280 240\" role=\"img\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\"><title>bifurcation</title><line x1=\"30\" y1=\"120\" x2=\"250\" y2=\"120\"/><polygon points=\"250,120 243.6,122.7 243.6,117.3\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\"/><line x1=\"140\" y1=\"210\" x2=\"140\" y2=\"30\"/><polygon points=\"140,30 142.7,36.4 137.3,36.4\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\"/><text x=\"245\" y=\"136\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">x₁</text><text x=\"148\" y=\"28\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">x₂</text><circle cx=\"140\" cy=\"120\" r=\"60\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 3\"/><polyline points=\"170,120 173.5,114.1 175.1,107.2 174.8,99.9 172.6,92.6 168.6,85.9 163.1,80 156.3,75.2 148.5,71.9 140,70.2 131.2,70.1 122.4,71.8 114,75 106.3,79.8 99.5,86 93.9,93.4 89.8,101.7 87.1,110.7 86.1,120 86.7,129.4 89,138.6 92.9,147.2 98.3,155 104.9,161.8 112.7,167.3 121.3,171.4 130.5,173.9 140,174.8 149.5,174 158.8,171.5 167.4,167.5 175.3,162 182.1,155.3 187.6,147.5 191.6,138.8 194.1,129.5 195,120\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/></svg>","figure_alt":"A closed orbit in the phase plane."},{"id":"limit-cycles-r2-q3","version":1,"round":2,"type":"mc","prompt":"A Hopf bifurcation creates or destroys","options":["a limit cycle as eigenvalues cross the imaginary axis","an equilibrium point","a saddle-node connection","multiple equilibria"],"correct_index":0,"explanation":"In a Hopf bifurcation, a complex conjugate pair of eigenvalues crosses the imaginary axis. A stable focus becomes unstable, and a limit cycle appears (or vice versa). This is distinct from saddle-node bifurcation of equilibria.","figure":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 300 180\" role=\"img\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\"><title>Hopf eigenvalues</title><line x1=\"20\" y1=\"90\" x2=\"280\" y2=\"90\"/><polygon points=\"280,90 273.6,92.7 273.6,87.3\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\"/><line x1=\"150\" y1=\"160\" x2=\"150\" y2=\"16\"/><polygon points=\"150,16 152.7,22.4 147.3,22.4\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\"/><text x=\"274\" y=\"106\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">Re</text><text x=\"158\" y=\"14\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">Im</text><line x1=\"145\" y1=\"41\" x2=\"155\" y2=\"51\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/><line x1=\"145\" y1=\"51\" x2=\"155\" y2=\"41\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/><line x1=\"145\" y1=\"129\" x2=\"155\" y2=\"139\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/><line x1=\"145\" y1=\"139\" x2=\"155\" y2=\"129\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/></svg>","figure_alt":"Eigenvalues on the imaginary axis."},{"id":"limit-cycles-r3-q1","version":1,"round":3,"type":"mc","prompt":"Relay (on-off) control can cause","options":["limit cycles due to switching","exponential stability","linear response","no oscillation"],"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Relay control switches between two states based on the error sign. This nonlinearity can create self-sustained oscillation (chattering or limit cycles) around the setpoint. The amplitude and frequency depend on the plant and relay hysteresis.","figure":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 280 240\" role=\"img\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\"><title>relay cycle</title><line x1=\"30\" y1=\"120\" x2=\"250\" y2=\"120\"/><polygon points=\"250,120 243.6,122.7 243.6,117.3\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\"/><line x1=\"140\" y1=\"210\" x2=\"140\" y2=\"30\"/><polygon points=\"140,30 142.7,36.4 137.3,36.4\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\"/><text x=\"245\" y=\"136\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">x₁</text><text x=\"148\" y=\"28\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">x₂</text><circle cx=\"140\" cy=\"120\" r=\"60\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 3\"/><polyline points=\"170,120 173.5,114.1 175.1,107.2 174.8,99.9 172.6,92.6 168.6,85.9 163.1,80 156.3,75.2 148.5,71.9 140,70.2 131.2,70.1 122.4,71.8 114,75 106.3,79.8 99.5,86 93.9,93.4 89.8,101.7 87.1,110.7 86.1,120 86.7,129.4 89,138.6 92.9,147.2 98.3,155 104.9,161.8 112.7,167.3 121.3,171.4 130.5,173.9 140,174.8 149.5,174 158.8,171.5 167.4,167.5 175.3,162 182.1,155.3 187.6,147.5 191.6,138.8 194.1,129.5 195,120\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/></svg>","figure_alt":"A closed orbit in the phase plane."},{"id":"limit-cycles-r3-q2","version":1,"round":3,"type":"mc","prompt":"The describing function method","options":["approximates a nonlinearity's response to sinusoidal input","finds exact solutions for limit cycles","applies only to linear systems","requires all nonlinearities to be saturation"],"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The describing function is the complex gain of a nonlinearity as a function of input amplitude. It approximates the fundamental harmonic response. Combined with the Nyquist plot, it predicts limit cycle amplitude and frequency.","figure":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 280 240\" role=\"img\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\"><title>describing function</title><line x1=\"30\" y1=\"120\" x2=\"250\" y2=\"120\"/><polygon points=\"250,120 243.6,122.7 243.6,117.3\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\"/><line x1=\"140\" y1=\"210\" x2=\"140\" y2=\"30\"/><polygon points=\"140,30 142.7,36.4 137.3,36.4\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\"/><text x=\"245\" y=\"136\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">x₁</text><text x=\"148\" y=\"28\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">x₂</text><circle cx=\"140\" cy=\"120\" r=\"60\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 3\"/><polyline points=\"170,120 173.5,114.1 175.1,107.2 174.8,99.9 172.6,92.6 168.6,85.9 163.1,80 156.3,75.2 148.5,71.9 140,70.2 131.2,70.1 122.4,71.8 114,75 106.3,79.8 99.5,86 93.9,93.4 89.8,101.7 87.1,110.7 86.1,120 86.7,129.4 89,138.6 92.9,147.2 98.3,155 104.9,161.8 112.7,167.3 121.3,171.4 130.5,173.9 140,174.8 149.5,174 158.8,171.5 167.4,167.5 175.3,162 182.1,155.3 187.6,147.5 191.6,138.8 194.1,129.5 195,120\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/></svg>","figure_alt":"A closed orbit in the phase plane."},{"id":"limit-cycles-r3-q3","version":1,"round":3,"type":"numeric","prompt":"A relay oscillation has period 2 s. The frequency is","answer":0.5,"unit":"Hz","tolerance":0.02,"explanation":"Frequency is the reciprocal of period: \\(f = 1/2 = 0.5\\) Hz. In rad/s, that is \\(\\omega = 2\\pi \\times 0.5 = \\pi\\) rad/s.","figure":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 280 240\" role=\"img\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\"><title>relay period</title><line x1=\"30\" y1=\"120\" x2=\"250\" y2=\"120\"/><polygon points=\"250,120 243.6,122.7 243.6,117.3\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\"/><line x1=\"140\" y1=\"210\" x2=\"140\" y2=\"30\"/><polygon points=\"140,30 142.7,36.4 137.3,36.4\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\"/><text x=\"245\" y=\"136\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">x₁</text><text x=\"148\" y=\"28\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">x₂</text><circle cx=\"140\" cy=\"120\" r=\"60\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 3\"/><polyline points=\"170,120 173.5,114.1 175.1,107.2 174.8,99.9 172.6,92.6 168.6,85.9 163.1,80 156.3,75.2 148.5,71.9 140,70.2 131.2,70.1 122.4,71.8 114,75 106.3,79.8 99.5,86 93.9,93.4 89.8,101.7 87.1,110.7 86.1,120 86.7,129.4 89,138.6 92.9,147.2 98.3,155 104.9,161.8 112.7,167.3 121.3,171.4 130.5,173.9 140,174.8 149.5,174 158.8,171.5 167.4,167.5 175.3,162 182.1,155.3 187.6,147.5 191.6,138.8 194.1,129.5 195,120\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/></svg>","figure_alt":"A closed orbit in the phase plane."},{"id":"limit-cycles-r4-q1","version":1,"round":4,"type":"mc","prompt":"A region of attraction is","options":["the set of initial conditions that converge to an equilibrium or limit cycle","the limit cycle itself","the set of all equilibria","the entire state space"],"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The region of attraction (basin of attraction) is the set of initial conditions from which trajectories converge to a particular attractor. Different attractors have different basins. The basin may not be the entire state space.","preview":["lyapunov"],"figure":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 280 240\" role=\"img\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\"><title>basin of attraction</title><line x1=\"30\" y1=\"120\" x2=\"250\" y2=\"120\"/><polygon points=\"250,120 243.6,122.7 243.6,117.3\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\"/><line x1=\"140\" y1=\"210\" x2=\"140\" y2=\"30\"/><polygon points=\"140,30 142.7,36.4 137.3,36.4\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\"/><text x=\"245\" y=\"136\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">x₁</text><text x=\"148\" y=\"28\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">x₂</text><circle cx=\"140\" cy=\"120\" r=\"60\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 3\"/><polyline points=\"170,120 173.5,114.1 175.1,107.2 174.8,99.9 172.6,92.6 168.6,85.9 163.1,80 156.3,75.2 148.5,71.9 140,70.2 131.2,70.1 122.4,71.8 114,75 106.3,79.8 99.5,86 93.9,93.4 89.8,101.7 87.1,110.7 86.1,120 86.7,129.4 89,138.6 92.9,147.2 98.3,155 104.9,161.8 112.7,167.3 121.3,171.4 130.5,173.9 140,174.8 149.5,174 158.8,171.5 167.4,167.5 175.3,162 182.1,155.3 187.6,147.5 191.6,138.8 194.1,129.5 195,120\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/></svg>","figure_alt":"A closed orbit in the phase plane."},{"id":"limit-cycles-r4-q2","version":1,"round":4,"type":"mc","prompt":"Multiple equilibria in a nonlinear system mean","options":["the long-term behavior depends on the initial condition","the system is linear","all trajectories go to the same point","there are no limit cycles"],"correct_index":0,"explanation":"With multiple equilibria, different initial conditions may converge to different resting states. The system has multiple basins of attraction. This is typical of nonlinear systems; linear systems have at most one equilibrium.","preview":["lyapunov"],"figure":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 280 240\" role=\"img\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\"><title>multiple equilibria</title><line x1=\"30\" y1=\"120\" x2=\"250\" y2=\"120\"/><polygon points=\"250,120 243.6,122.7 243.6,117.3\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\"/><line x1=\"140\" y1=\"210\" x2=\"140\" y2=\"30\"/><polygon points=\"140,30 142.7,36.4 137.3,36.4\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\"/><text x=\"245\" y=\"136\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">x₁</text><text x=\"148\" y=\"28\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">x₂</text><circle cx=\"140\" cy=\"120\" r=\"60\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 3\"/><polyline points=\"170,120 173.5,114.1 175.1,107.2 174.8,99.9 172.6,92.6 168.6,85.9 163.1,80 156.3,75.2 148.5,71.9 140,70.2 131.2,70.1 122.4,71.8 114,75 106.3,79.8 99.5,86 93.9,93.4 89.8,101.7 87.1,110.7 86.1,120 86.7,129.4 89,138.6 92.9,147.2 98.3,155 104.9,161.8 112.7,167.3 121.3,171.4 130.5,173.9 140,174.8 149.5,174 158.8,171.5 167.4,167.5 175.3,162 182.1,155.3 187.6,147.5 191.6,138.8 194.1,129.5 195,120\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/></svg>","figure_alt":"A phase portrait with trajectories."},{"id":"limit-cycles-r4-q3","version":1,"round":4,"type":"mc","prompt":"Poincar\\u00e9 map analysis of periodic orbits","options":["samples the trajectory at fixed intervals and studies the resulting map","uses only linear analysis","finds all equilibria","applies only in one dimension"],"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The Poincar\\u00e9 map records where a trajectory crosses a surface of section. For a limit cycle, this gives a fixed point of the map. Stability of the limit cycle relates to stability of this fixed point. It extends analysis to higher dimensions.","preview":["gen-gain-phase"],"figure":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 280 240\" role=\"img\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\"><title>Poincare map</title><line x1=\"30\" y1=\"120\" x2=\"250\" y2=\"120\"/><polygon points=\"250,120 243.6,122.7 243.6,117.3\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\"/><line x1=\"140\" y1=\"210\" x2=\"140\" y2=\"30\"/><polygon points=\"140,30 142.7,36.4 137.3,36.4\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\"/><text x=\"245\" y=\"136\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">x₁</text><text x=\"148\" y=\"28\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">x₂</text><circle cx=\"140\" cy=\"120\" r=\"60\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 3\"/><polyline points=\"170,120 173.5,114.1 175.1,107.2 174.8,99.9 172.6,92.6 168.6,85.9 163.1,80 156.3,75.2 148.5,71.9 140,70.2 131.2,70.1 122.4,71.8 114,75 106.3,79.8 99.5,86 93.9,93.4 89.8,101.7 87.1,110.7 86.1,120 86.7,129.4 89,138.6 92.9,147.2 98.3,155 104.9,161.8 112.7,167.3 121.3,171.4 130.5,173.9 140,174.8 149.5,174 158.8,171.5 167.4,167.5 175.3,162 182.1,155.3 187.6,147.5 191.6,138.8 194.1,129.5 195,120\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/></svg>","figure_alt":"A closed orbit in the phase plane."}]}